+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+# [MP8] Spawn point pool (2026-08-17)
+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+#
+# **Why not use the generic fill-in?** (the node-name whitelist path in network_manager)
+# probe41 tested in practice that this minigame's container name `PlayerSpawnPositions` is indeed on the whitelist and can be filled up to 8,
+# but the generic algorithm uses "the midpoint of the line between two adjacent points", and vanilla's 4 points are placed **around the structure in the middle of the arena**
+# — the lines pass right through it, and 2 of the 4 midpoints land in holes cut out of the navigation mesh.
+#     fill 2 (8.35, 0.79)    fill 3 (9.79, -0.21)
+# It's the same pitfall as Manufacture Gun v1 (HANDOFF pitfall #25 / T21). So `mp8_owns_spawns` is set here
+# to keep the generic fill-in out entirely, and this minigame manages its own spawn points instead.
+#
+# **Pool rules (set by the user on 2026-08-17)**
+#   1. Every player count uses the pool — the user explicitly chose this, **no longer keeping "4-player matches identical to vanilla value for value"**.
+#   2. Pool order = **order added** (the 4 from the vanilla scene are 1~4, and the user appended the rest one by one in the manual editing mode).
+#   3. Each player count only enables **the first N**. Fewer players use a small inner cluster (everyone close together, tight pacing), and it only spreads out when full.
+#   4. The draw is random, but picked points are guaranteed a minimum gap, to avoid random messy matches with everyone crammed together.
+#
+# ⚠️ The built-in defaults **don't bake in coordinates**. The pool's base is simply the actual values of the 4 Markers already in the scene,
+#    read at runtime. Copying coordinates is something this project has been burned by (HANDOFF stresses this repeatedly), so avoid it whenever possible.
+# ⚠️ After adding points to the pool **this table has to be updated too**, otherwise the new points can never be drawn.
+# On 2026-08-17 the user finalized 25 hand-placed points, but the old table only enabled the first 20 for 8 players → points 21~25 were dead points.
+# Now 8 players = the whole pool of 25. Redundancy (enabled count ÷ needed count, where needed = players + devices) stays around 2x.
+const MP8_POOL_BY_COUNT: Dictionary = {
+	4: 10,
+	5: 13,
+	6: 16,
+	7: 20,
+	8: 25,
+}
