+# After which kill a device stays pinned on the person and won't come off (set by the user on 2026-08-17, and revised once the same day after real gameplay).
+#
+# **The goal is "only 1 device left on the field when the last 2 players go 1v1"** — so retirement has to happen on
+# "the kill that leaves exactly 2 players", so threshold = total players − 2.
+#   6 players → 4th kill    7 players → 5th kill    8 players → 6th kill
+#
+# ⚠️ It used to be hard-coded to 4, derived from "guarantee only one device is left for the showdown" — that condition does hold, but it **retired too early**.
+#    In an 8-player match there are still 4 players left after the 4th kill, and the remaining device has to chase 4 people, which takes a long time.
+#    The user rejected it after real gameplay ("the stopped one quit moving while there were still four people on the field").
+#    **A rule that makes sense doesn't mean the timing is right**. What matters here is pacing, not the inequality holding.
+# [MP8] Write placement/name/score to the log at results time (doesn't change any scores, purely for checking afterwards).
+# Same format as Junk Platform's _mp8_log_scores, so the two A-group minigames are easy to compare side by side.
+func _mp8_log_scores() -> void :
+
+	var ranked: Array = player_scores.keys()
+	ranked.sort_custom(func(a, b): return player_scores[a] > player_scores[b])
+
+	var parts: PackedStringArray = []
+	for i in ranked.size():
+		var nid = ranked[i]
+		var nm: String = "id%d" % nid
+		if PlayerManager.player_presences.has(nid):
+			nm = String(PlayerManager.player_presences[nid].network_name)
+		parts.append("#%d %s %d pts" % [i + 1, nm, player_scores[nid]])
+
+	print("[MP8] Spine Breaker results (%d players present / %d dead): %s" % [
+		player_count, dead_players.size(), " / ".join(parts)
+	])
