+# Personality difference between the two devices (set by the user on 2026-08-17)
+#   **Device 1 = baseline** (10 sec fuse, normal speed)  **Device 2 = meaner** (9 sec fuse, 1.2x speed)
+# All the difference goes on device 2 — device 1 is the original one from the scene, and "only touch what we added" is this project's standing rule.
+#
+# ⭐ **Unequal fuses are what break the lockstep, not the speed difference.**
+#   Time of death = time dispatched + fuse, regardless of how fast it chases (the fuse starts burning in choose_new_target,
+#   so travel time is included. At 7 m/s crossing the whole arena takes 4~5 sec at most, so a 10 sec fuse always covers it).
+#   So however fast the two devices run, they kill in the same second. With 10/9 the cycles become 13 sec / 12 sec,
+#   drifting 1 more second apart each round → deaths go from paired to alternating, and placement no longer depends on which await returns first.
+#
+# The speed difference changes something else — **how early you get caught**, in other words how many chances you get to break free —
+# the fuse doesn't stop when it's thrown off, so getting latched early = you can throw it and run. Getting touched only after the fuse has burned out = executed on the spot.
+#
+# ⚠️ The 10 sec fuse applies to 4-player matches too (the vanilla main scene uses 20 sec). The user explicitly asked for it, in the same revision as the pool.
+# ⚠️ Index = device number − 1. Devices beyond the table (if we ever really go to 3~4 devices) all fall back to device 1's value.
+# ⚠️⚠️ Settled on 2026-08-18 from the user's 6-player **match with real players** — **device 1 20 sec / device 2 15 sec.**
+# (They first said "too short, add 3 seconds", then immediately changed it to these two numbers — this one is what counts.)
+#
+# What's increased is the **fuse** and nothing else. Spine Breaker has no round timer (pure elimination, it only ends when 1 player is left),
+# so the only "time" players can feel is this — the escape window between a device latching on and the execution.
+#
+# Device 1 at 20 sec = **back to the vanilla main scene value** (the earlier cut to 10 sec was set on 2026-08-17 and overturned by real gameplay).
+# Device 2 at 15 sec is still meaner, and **the two differ by 5 sec** (it used to be only 1 sec) —
+# the offset is maintained by the fuses being unequal, and the bigger the gap the less likely they fall into lockstep.
+# Cycle = fuse + MP8_DEVICE_WAIT → 23 sec vs 17 sec, drifting 6 sec apart each round (it used to be 2 sec).
+#
+# 🩸 This also removes a race along the way. Before, 10/9 only differed by 1 sec, and in break_spine_rpc
+# "await 0.5 + await 0.5" is also exactly 1 sec — when two devices were on the same person,
+# the moment the later one did connect(spine_broken) collided with the earlier one's emit, and connecting a step late meant it never got the signal.
+# With a 5 sec gap these windows no longer line up (the real fix is in thrown_state and break_spine_state,
+# this just breaks up the coincidence along the way).
+const MP8_DEVICE_FUSE: Array[float] = [20.0, 15.0]
+const MP8_DEVICE_SPEED: Array[float] = [1.0, 1.2]
+
+# How long to wait before heading out again after a kill (set by the user on 2026-08-17 — 2 sec and 3 sec).
+#
+# ⚠️⚠️ **Which device gets which number isn't arbitrary. Swap them and the offset cancels out.**
+# Cycle = fuse + this wait, and the **cycle difference** between the two is how far they can drift apart each round.
+#     Dev 1 3 sec / Dev 2 2 sec → 13 sec vs 11 sec → drifts **2 sec** apart each round (1→3→5→7) ✅
+#     Dev 1 2 sec / Dev 2 3 sec → 12 sec vs 12 sec → **same cycle, locked at a 1 sec gap forever** ❌
+# The latter is worse than no change. Without the change there's at least the 1 sec drift of 13 vs 12, but swapped, the drift drops straight to zero.
+# So the slower device (device 1, with the longer fuse) gets the longer wait and the faster one gets the shorter wait, so the differences add up.
+const MP8_DEVICE_WAIT: Array[float] = [3.0, 2.0]
+
+# Body color (set by the user on 2026-08-17 — paint the added device red so it's easy to tell at a glance which one is meaner).
+# Color.WHITE = no tint. albedo_color is **multiplied** onto the texture, so don't use pure (1,0,0).
+# That would multiply away the texture's green and blue channels entirely and turn the body into a flat red blob with all detail lost.
+const MP8_DEVICE_TINT: Array[Color] = [Color.WHITE, Color(1.0, 0.25, 0.2)]
